      title: "Labor Organizes",
      body: `## The Great Railroad Strike

As you saw in AMH 210, the first great national labor upheaval came in July 1877, when railroad workers protesting wage cuts walked off the job, beginning in West Virginia. The strike spread to Baltimore, Pittsburgh, Chicago, St. Louis, and beyond, paralyzing much of the nation's rail traffic. In Pittsburgh, strikers and their supporters, after militia fired on a crowd, burned railroad property. President Hayes sent federal troops, and the strike was crushed. About a hundred people died. The **Great Railroad Strike** frightened middle-class Americans, who saw in it the specter of the Paris Commune of 1871. States strengthened their militias and built armories in city centers, many of which still stand.

## The Knights of Labor

The most important labor organization of the 1880s was the **Noble and Holy Order of the Knights of Labor**, founded in Philadelphia in 1869 as a secret society of garment cutters. Under the leadership of **Terence V. Powderly**, who became Grand Master Workman in 1879, it abandoned secrecy and grew rapidly. The Knights sought to unite all "producers"—skilled and unskilled, men and women, Black and white—in a single organization. They excluded only bankers, lawyers, gamblers, liquor dealers, and stockbrokers—and, shamefully, Chinese workers, whom many Knights wanted excluded from the country.

The Knights' goals went far beyond higher wages. They sought to replace the wage system with a "cooperative commonwealth," in which workers would own the enterprises they worked in. They advocated the eight-hour day, an end to child labor, equal pay for women, a graduated income tax, and government ownership of railroads and telegraphs. Powderly disliked strikes, preferring arbitration and cooperation. But in 1885, Knights on Jay Gould's Southwestern railroads won a strike that forced Gould to restore wage cuts. The victory sent membership soaring, to perhaps 700,000 in 1886.

## Haymarket

The year 1886 saw unprecedented labor unrest—over 1,400 strikes involving some 400,000 workers—much of it centered on a national campaign for the **eight-hour day**, set to culminate in strikes on May 1. Chicago was the center of the movement, and of a small but vocal anarchist movement, largely of German immigrants.

On May 3, police killed several strikers at the McCormick Harvester plant. Anarchists called a protest meeting the next evening at **Haymarket Square**. The meeting was peaceful, and the mayor, who attended, left early. But as police moved in to disperse the dwindling crowd, someone threw a bomb, killing one policeman instantly; in the gunfire that followed, mostly from police, seven policemen and at least four civilians died, and many were wounded.

The bomber was never identified. But eight anarchists were tried for murder, on the theory that their speeches and writings had incited the unknown bomber. All were convicted in a trial marked by prejudice; seven were sentenced to death. Four were hanged in November 1887; one killed himself in his cell. In 1893 the governor of Illinois, **John Peter Altgeld**, pardoned the three survivors, declaring that the trial had been unjust, a decision that ended his political career. The **Haymarket affair** set off a national Red Scare, discredited the eight-hour movement, and devastated the Knights of Labor, who were unfairly associated with anarchism. Membership collapsed. May 1, May Day, became an international workers' holiday in memory of the Haymarket martyrs—everywhere except the United States.

## The American Federation of Labor

As the Knights declined, a different kind of organization rose. In 1886, a group of craft unions founded the **American Federation of Labor**, led by **Samuel Gompers**, a cigar maker who had emigrated from London as a boy. The AFL was a federation of national unions of skilled workers, organized by craft: carpenters, printers, machinists, and so on. Gompers rejected the Knights' utopian goals and political radicalism in favor of "**pure and simple unionism**": higher wages, shorter hours, and better working conditions, won through collective bargaining and strikes. Asked what labor wanted, Gompers answered in an 1893 speech: "We want more schoolhouses and less jails; more books and less arsenals; more learning and less vice; more constant work and less crime; more leisure and less greed; more justice and less revenge."

The AFL's focus on skilled workers made it durable—it survived the depressions that destroyed broader organizations—and it grew to over a million members by 1904. But it left most workers, the unskilled, women, Black workers, and new immigrants, unorganized. Many AFL unions explicitly excluded Black workers, and the AFL supported immigration restriction.

## Homestead

In 1892, at Andrew Carnegie's steel works at **Homestead**, Pennsylvania, the skilled workers' union, the Amalgamated Association of Iron and Steel Workers, came into conflict with Carnegie's partner and manager, **Henry Clay Frick**. Carnegie, who had publicly defended workers' right to organize, was in Scotland, but he supported Frick's determination to break the union. When the union refused to accept wage cuts, Frick locked the workers out, surrounded the plant with a fence topped with barbed wire, and hired 300 armed guards from the Pinkerton Detective Agency to protect strikebreakers.

On July 6, 1892, as the Pinkertons approached on barges up the Monongahela River, thousands of workers and townspeople met them on the riverbank. In the battle that followed, seven workers and three Pinkertons were killed; the Pinkertons surrendered and were forced to run a gauntlet of angry townspeople. The governor sent 8,000 militiamen, who took control of the town, and the plant reopened with strikebreakers. Two weeks later, an anarchist, Alexander Berkman, who had no connection to the strikers, shot and stabbed Frick in his office; Frick survived, and public sympathy swung away from the workers. The strike was broken, and the union was destroyed. Carnegie's mills would remain nonunion for forty years.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did the AFL survive while the Knights of Labor collapsed? What did the AFL's strategy gain, and what did it give up?`,
